use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Virtualisation:
    description:
        Virtualisation draws only the part of a long list that is on screen,
        plus a little extra, so a list of a million items is as quick as a list
        of fifty.

    window:
        The items on screen are drawn, along with some just above and below,
        so the user doesn't see blank space while scrolling and the next items
        are being drawn.

    extent:
        The scrollbar reflects the whole list, not just the part that is drawn,
        so its size and position are accurate.

    measurement:
        Either every item is the same, known height, or each item is measured
        as it is drawn and the estimates are corrected as it goes. Correcting
        an estimate never makes what the user is looking at jump.

    accessibility:
        Screen readers and other assistive tools are told the real number of
        items, and each item's real position in the whole list, not just among
        the items currently drawn. How this is done is up to the chosen
        library.

    focusRetention:
        The item the user is focused on stays drawn even after it scrolls off
        screen, so focus isn't lost. When a key like End or Page Down moves
        focus to an item that isn't drawn yet, the list scrolls to it first,
        then focus lands on it.

    nonGoals:
        - Not on by default. It makes measuring and focus harder to get right,
            so a list short enough to draw in full should just be drawn in full
